  "finalReply": "Created `/app/polyglot/main.rs` as a Rust/C++ polyglot.\n\nHow it works:\n- `/*` / `/*` / `*/` exploits nested-comment divergence: after the header Rust is still inside a block comment while C++ is outside, so `#include`s are hidden from `rustc` but seen by `g++`.\n- `#if 0` / `*/` then exposes Rust code (C++ skips it, Rust exits the comment).\n- `// \\` + newline + `/*` uses line-splice divergence: C++ joins the first two lines into one `//` comment (so no block comment starts), while Rust sees `// \\` as one line comment and `/*` as a new block comment start. Then `#endif` ends C++ skipping while staying hidden from Rust inside that block comment.\n- Final `// */` closes the Rust block comment while being just a line comment in C++.\n- Rust region avoids `/*`, trailing `\\`, and `'` (so C++ phase 2/3 still finds `#endif`); C++ region avoids `/*`/`*/` (so Rust nesting is undisturbed).\n\nBoth sides implement the same arbitrary-precision iterative Fibonacci (base-1e9 limbs, `f(0)=1, f(1)=1`), so large `N` also matches.\n\nVerified:\n- `rustc 1.75.0`: `rustc /app/polyglot/main.rs` succeeds\n- `g++`: `g++ -x c++ /app/polyglot/main.rs -o /app/polyglot/cmain` succeeds\n- `main N` and `cmain N` agree for N = 0,1,2,3,4,5,10,20,50,92,100,200 (e.g. N=10 → `89`, N=200 → `453973694165307953197296969697410619233826`).",
